Group 1: Tropical Fruits
- Mango, Pineapple, Papaya, Guava, Dragonfruit
These fruits all originate from tropical regions and thrive in warm, humid climates. They are known for their sweet and juicy flavors, and are excellent sources of vitamins and minerals including vitamin C, vitamin A, potassium, and fiber.
Mangoes are native to India and Southeast Asia, and are rich in vitamins A and C, as well as folate and fiber. Pineapples are native to South America, and are a good source of vitamin C and manganese, which is important for bone health. Papayas are native to Central and South America, and are rich in vitamin C, vitamin A, folate, and potassium. Guavas are native to Mexico and Central America, and are a good source of vitamin C, fiber, and antioxidants. Dragonfruit, also known as pitaya, is native to South America and is a good source of vitamin C, iron, and magnesium.
Group 2: Kiwi
- Kiwi
Kiwi is a fruit that originates from China and is now grown in many parts of the world, including New Zealand. Kiwis are a good source of vitamin C, vitamin K, and potassium. They are also high in antioxidants and fiber.
Group 3: None
Based on their origin and nutrient profiles, these six fruits can be categorized into two groups - tropical fruits and kiwi. There is not enough information to categorize them into three groups. <|endoftext|>
