Sure, congruence in geometry refers to the property of two figures having the same size and shape. In other words, if we can superimpose one figure onto the other so that they completely overlap, then the figures are considered congruent. 

There are several criteria for determining whether two figures are congruent, such as side-side-side (SSS), side-angle-side (SAS), angle-side-angle (ASA), and hypotenuse-leg (HL). These criteria essentially state that if certain combinations of corresponding sides and angles are equal between two figures, then the figures must be congruent.

Congruence is an important concept in geometry because it allows us to make precise statements about the relationships between different figures. For example, if we know that two triangles are congruent, we can conclude that all corresponding angles and sides between the triangles are equal. <|endoftext|>
