The French language has its origins in the vernacular Latin spoken by the Roman Empire's soldiers, settlers and merchants in Gaul, which comprised modern-day France, Switzerland, and Belgium. Over time, this language evolved into Old French, which was spoken from the 9th to the 14th century. Middle French was spoken from the 14th to the 17th century, while Modern French developed from the 17th century onwards. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
