<|user|>
List 3 specific historical landmarks along the route of the Tour de France and describe their significance in relation to the race.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. Mont Ventoux - This mountain is a legendary climb in the Tour de France, and has been included in the race since 1951. It is known for its barren and windswept terrain, and has been the site of many memorable battles between riders. In 1967, British cyclist Tom Simpson died on the slopes of Mont Ventoux, making it a poignant and tragic part of Tour history.
2. Alpe d'Huez - This mountain is another iconic climb in the Tour de France, and has been included in the race 30 times since 1952. It is known for its 21 hairpin turns, which have become a symbol of the race. The climb is often decisive in determining the winner of the Tour, and has been the site of many dramatic moments over the years.
3. Arc de Triomphe - This famous monument in Paris is the traditional finish line of the Tour de France. It is an iconic symbol of the city, and has been the site of many thrilling sprint finishes over the years. The winner of the Tour is crowned on the podium in front of the Arc de Triomphe, making it a fitting and symbolic end to the race. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the different classifications of jerseys worn by riders in the Tour de France and what they represent?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, there are four different jerseys that riders can wear during the Tour de France. 
1. Yellow Jersey - This is the most iconic jersey and is worn by the rider who has the lowest overall time after each stage. It represents the leader of the race.
2. Green Jersey - This jersey is worn by the rider who has accumulated the most points throughout the race. Points are awarded for finishing high in each stage as well as for intermediate sprints. The green jersey represents the leader of the sprint competition.
3. Polka Dot Jersey - This jersey is worn by the rider who has accumulated the most points in the mountain stages of the race. Points are awarded for reaching the summit of designated climbs first. The polka dot jersey represents the leader of the King of the Mountains competition.
4. White Jersey - This jersey is worn by the best young rider in the race, which is defined as a rider who is under 25 years old at the start of the year. It represents the future star of the race. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do teams work in the Tour de France and what is their role in supporting their lead rider?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Teams play a crucial role in the Tour de France, and typically consist of 8 riders. Each team will have a designated leader, or "GC contender," who they will work to support throughout the race. The other members of the team will ride in a variety of roles, such as protecting the leader from crashes and wind, fetching food and water, and setting pace on the front of the peloton. 

One key tactic used by teams is the "domestique" system, where one or more riders sacrifice their own chances of winning to help the team leader. These riders will ride at the front of the peloton to set a high tempo, chase down breakaways, and protect the leader from wind. They will also ride back to the team car to collect food and water for the leader and offer assistance if needed. 

Overall, the success of a team is judged by the performance of their leader, so they will do everything possible to ensure that their leader has the best chance of winning the race. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


