How can I efficiently create a Python function that takes in a list of integers and outputs a new list with the same elements in reverse order? The function should have a time complexity of O(n/2) and should exclude any non-integer elements from the input list. Additionally, if there are no valid integers in the input list, the function should return an empty list.
[formula]
reverse_list = lambda l: [i for i in reversed(l) if isinstance(i, int)] if any(isinstance(i, int) for i in l) else []
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your solution is correct! Here's an explanation of how it works:
The lambda function takes in a list `l` and uses a list comprehension to create a new list with the same elements in reverse order. The `reversed()` function is used to iterate over the list in reverse order. The `if` statement filters out any non-integer elements using the `isinstance()` function.
The `isinstance()` function checks if an object is an instance of a particular class. In this case, we're checking if each element of the list is an instance of the `int` class.
The `any()` function is used to check if there are any integer elements in the list. If there are no integer elements in the list, the lambda function returns an empty list.
The time complexity of this function is O(n/2) because it only needs to iterate over half of the list to reverse it. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


