<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue data structure?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A stack is a data structure that follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle, meaning that the last item added to the stack is the first one to be removed. A queue, on the other hand, follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle, meaning that the first item added to the queue is the first one to be removed. In a stack, operations such as push (adding an item) and pop (removing an item) occur at the same end of the data structure, while in a queue, they occur at opposite ends. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


