You need to organize a group of people to sit at a round table, ensuring that no two people who dislike each other sit next to each other. Additionally, you must make sure that at least one person wearing a red shirt and one person wearing a blue shirt sit at the table. Can you come up with a mathematical formula or algorithm to automate this process for different table sizes and numbers of people, while ensuring that the seating arrangement is symmetrical?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, I can come up with an algorithm to automate this process.
1. Create a list of all the people who will be sitting at the table.
2. Assign each person a number from 1 to n, where n is the number of people.
3. Create a matrix with n rows and n columns, representing the table.
4. Initialize the matrix with zeros.
5. For each pair of people who dislike each other, set the corresponding entry in the matrix to 1.
6. Create a list of all possible seating arrangements, using permutations of the numbers 1 to n.
7. For each seating arrangement, check if any two adjacent numbers in the arrangement correspond to people who dislike each other. If so, discard the arrangement.
8. If a valid seating arrangement is found, check if there is at least one person wearing a red shirt and one person wearing a blue shirt. If not, discard the arrangement.
9. Once a valid seating arrangement is found, mirror it across the vertical axis to ensure symmetry.
10. Output the final seating arrangement.
This algorithm can be easily adapted for different table sizes and numbers of people by adjusting the size of the matrix and the range of numbers assigned to each person. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
