To modify the algorithm to ensure that no three people who dislike each other sit at adjacent seats, we can follow a similar approach. 
1. Create a list of all the people who will be sitting at the table.
2. Assign each person a number from 1 to n, where n is the number of people.
3. Create a matrix with n rows and n columns, representing the table.
4. Initialize the matrix with zeros.
5. For each pair of people who dislike each other, set the corresponding entry in the matrix to 1.
6. Create a list of all possible seating arrangements, using permutations of the numbers 1 to n.
7. For each seating arrangement, check if any two adjacent numbers in the arrangement correspond to people who dislike each other. If so, discard the arrangement.
8. For each group of three adjacent seats, check if there are three people who dislike each other. If so, discard the arrangement.
9. Check if there is at least one person wearing a red shirt and one person wearing a blue shirt. If not, discard the arrangement.
10. Once a valid seating arrangement is found, mirror it across the vertical axis to ensure symmetry.
11. Output the final seating arrangement.

This modification requires an additional step to check for groups of three adjacent seats containing people who dislike each other. We can use a sliding window approach to check each group of three adjacent seats in the seating arrangement. If all three people in a group dislike each other, then we discard the arrangement. This modification ensures that no three people who dislike each other will sit together. <|endoftext|>
