What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous communication in computer science?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Synchronous communication refers to a type of communication where the sender and receiver are synchronized i.e., they work in a time-bound system where data transfer happens at a fixed rate. In this case, both parties need to be active at the same time for communication to happen.

Asynchronous communication, on the other hand, refers to a type of communication where the sender and receiver are not synchronized, and data transfer can happen at any time. In this case, both parties do not need to be active at the same time for communication to take place. 

To explain further, let's consider an example of sending an email. If you send an email, you don't expect an immediate response - the recipient might check their email after some time and respond accordingly. This is an example of asynchronous communication. However, if you're having a live chat with someone, you'd expect real-time responses, and hence it's an example of synchronous communication. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


