Here's a sample code in C# that generates a random sentence with alliteration:
```
using System;
class Program
{
 static void Main(string[] args)
 {
 string[] subjects = { "The cat", "The dog", "The bird", "The elephant", "The frog" };
 string[] verbs = { "jumped", "jogged", "jived", "jolted", "jiggled" };
 string[] objects = { "jovially", "jaggedly", "jarringly", "jubilantly", "jadedly" };
 Random rand = new Random();
 string sentence = subjects[rand.Next(0, subjects.Length)] + " " + verbs[rand.Next(0, verbs.Length)] + " " + objects[rand.Next(0, objects.Length)];
 Console.WriteLine(sentence);
 }
}
```
This code creates three arrays containing alliterative words for subjects, verbs, and objects. It then generates a random sentence by selecting a random word from each array and concatenating them together. The result is a sentence with alliteration, such as "The bird jogged jubilantly." <|endoftext|>
