Sure, I can help you with that! Here's the Swift function that retrieves the name of the equipment used for catching the ball by analyzing the table of available equipment and their materials:
```
func getEquipmentName() -> String {
 let availableEquipment = [
 BaseballEquipment(name: "Catcher's Mitt", purpose: "To catch and secure the ball", material: "Leather"),
 BaseballEquipment(name: "Bat", purpose: "To hit the ball", material: "Wood or Metal"),
 BaseballEquipment(name: "Helmet", purpose: "To protect the head", material: "Plastic and Foam"),
 BaseballEquipment(name: "Glove", purpose: "To catch the ball", material: "Leather"),
 BaseballEquipment(name: "Cleats", purpose: "To have a better grip on the field", material: "Rubber or Metal")
 ]
 
 let catcherEquipment = availableEquipment.filter({$0.purpose == "To catch and secure the ball"})
 let suitableEquipment = catcherEquipment.filter({$0.material == "Leather"})
 
 guard let equipment = suitableEquipment.first else {
 return "No suitable equipment found"
 }
 
 return equipment.name
}
```
This function first creates an array of available equipment with their names, purposes, and materials. Then, it filters the equipment that is suitable for catching the ball by looking for the equipment with the purpose of "To catch and secure the ball". Finally, it filters out the equipment that is not made of leather and returns the name of the first suitable equipment found. If no suitable equipment is found, it returns a message saying "No suitable equipment found". <|endoftext|>
