| Idiom/Phrase | Meaning | Origin | Evolution over time |
|--------------|---------|--------|---------------------|
| A penny for your thoughts | Asking someone what they are thinking about | Origin unknown | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Break a leg | Good luck | Origin unknown | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Bite the bullet | To endure a painful or difficult situation | Originated in the 1800s when soldiers would bite a bullet during surgery to endure the pain | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Cat got your tongue | Asking someone why they are not speaking | Originated in the 1800s when a whip was used to punish sailors who spoke out of turn, and the whip was called "the cat" | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Hit the nail on the head | To be correct or accurate | Originated in the 1500s when carpenters would hit a nail on the head to secure it in place | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Kick the bucket | To die | Originated in the 1600s when people would stand on a bucket with a noose around their neck and kick the bucket away to commit suicide | The phrase has evolved to be used more casually to mean "to die" |
| Piece of cake | Something that is easy to do | Originated in the early 1900s when cakes were given as prizes for winning contests | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
| Spill the beans | To reveal a secret | Originated in ancient Greece when beans were used in voting, and if someone spilled the beans, it would reveal the outcome of the vote | The phrase has remained unchanged over time |
