<|asst|>
Speed is a scalar quantity that describes how fast an object is moving, regardless of its direction. Velocity is a vector quantity that describes both the speed and direction of motion. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How does air resistance affect the speed of falling objects?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Air resistance, also known as drag, acts against the motion of a falling object and reduces its speed. As an object falls faster, air resistance increases until it balances the force of gravity, resulting in a constant terminal velocity. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Why do some materials conduct electricity better than others?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Materials that conduct electricity well have free electrons that are able to move easily through the material when an electric field is applied. These materials typically have a low resistance to the flow of electric charge. On the other hand, materials with tightly bound electrons or no free electrons, like insulators, have a high resistance to electric charge flow. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
