<|end|>
<|user|>
How do trees communicate with each other?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Trees communicate with each other through a network of fungi that live in the soil called mycorrhizae. These fungi connect the roots of different trees, allowing them to exchange nutrients and information about environmental conditions. Trees also release chemicals into the air and soil that can signal other trees to defend against predators or adjust their growth patterns. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


