How does the brain distinguish between different languages?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The brain distinguishes between different languages through a process called language lateralization. This is when certain areas of the brain are activated when we hear, read, or speak a particular language. For example, the left hemisphere of the brain is typically more active when processing language, and it has been found that different languages activate different regions of the brain. Additionally, the brain uses context clues such as sentence structure and vocabulary to differentiate between languages. <|endoftext|>
