Why do some people have photographic memories while others don't?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
While there is no concrete answer to this question, research suggests that it may have to do with differences in brain structure and function. Studies have shown that individuals with highly superior autobiographical memory (HSAM), which is often referred to as "photographic memory," have certain structural and functional differences in their brains compared to those without HSAM. Specifically, they tend to have larger caudate nucleus volumes, more efficient connectivity between brain regions, and greater white matter integrity in the temporal lobe. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
