How do scientists measure the age of the universe?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Scientists use a variety of methods to estimate the age of the universe, but one of the most commonly used methods is based on the cosmic microwave background radiation (CMB). The CMB is essentially leftover radiation from the Big Bang, and by studying its properties, scientists can make estimates about the age of the universe. Specifically, they look for patterns in the CMB that indicate how long it has been since the radiation was emitted. Other methods for estimating the age of the universe include studying the brightness and distance of certain stars and galaxies, and measuring the rate at which the universe is expanding. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
