<|user|>
I have a list of words in an Excel table and I need to check if they follow the correct spelling rule. For example, the word "imossible" seems to be spelled incorrectly.
Can you write a MATLAB code that will take in the list of words and output a JSON data with a Boolean value indicating whether each word follows the correct spelling rule or not? The rule is that no two consonants can appear together without a vowel in between.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a MATLAB code that can help you achieve this:
```matlab
% Read the Excel table
table = readtable('word_list.xlsx');
% Extract the words from the table
words = table.Word;
% Initialize an empty struct to store the results
results = struct;
% Loop through each word and check if it follows the spelling rule
for i = 1:length(words)
 word = lower(words{i});
 is_valid = true;
 for j = 1:length(word)-1
 if ~isvowel(word(j)) && ~isvowel(word(j+1)) && word(j) ~= word(j+1)
 is_valid = false;
 break;
 end
 end
 results(i).word = word;
 results(i).is_valid = is_valid;
end
% Convert the results struct to a JSON data
json_data = jsonencode(results);
% Write the JSON data to a file
fid = fopen('spelling_results.json', 'w');
fprintf(fid, '%s', json_data);
fclose(fid);
% Function to check if a character is a vowel
function is_vowel = isvowel(c)
 vowels = 'aeiou';
 is_vowel = any(c == vowels);
end
```
This code reads in an Excel table with a column of words and loops through each word to check if it follows the spelling rule. It then stores the results in a struct and converts it to a JSON data. Finally, it writes the JSON data to a file called "spelling_results.json". The function "isvowel" is used to check if a character is a vowel. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Thank you for the code. Is there a way to modify it to check if a word contains any unnecessary vowels, i.e., a vowel that does not contribute to the pronunciation of the word, such as the "e" at the end of "impossible"?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, we can modify the code to check for unnecessary vowels in addition to the spelling rule. Here's a modified version of the previous code:
```matlab
% Read the Excel table
table = readtable('word_list.xlsx');
% Extract the words from the table
words = table.Word;
% Initialize an empty struct to store the results
results = struct;
% Loop through each word and check if it follows the spelling rule and has no unnecessary vowels
for i = 1:length(words)
 word = lower(words{i});
 is_valid_spelling = true;
 is_valid_vowels = true;
 lastchar = '';
 vowelcount = 0;
 for j = 1:length(word)
 if ~isvowel(word(j)) && ~isvowel(lastchar) && lastchar ~= ''
 is_valid_spelling = false;
 break;
 elseif isvowel(word(j))
 vowelcount = vowelcount + 1;
 lastchar = 'v';
 if vowelcount > 2
 is_valid_vowels = false;
 break;
 end
 else
 lastchar = 'c';
 vowelcount = 0;
 end
 end
 results(i).word = word;
 results(i).is_valid_spelling = is_valid_spelling;
 results(i).is_valid_vowels = is_valid_vowels;
end
% Convert the results struct to a JSON data
json_data = jsonencode(results);
% Write the JSON data to a file
fid = fopen('spelling_and_vowel_results.json', 'w');
fprintf(fid, '%s', json_data);
fclose(fid);
% Function to check if a character is a vowel
function is_vowel = isvowel(c)
 vowels = 'aeiouy';
 is_vowel = any(c == vowels);
end
```
This code has two additional checks. The first check ensures that no two consonants appear together without a vowel in between. The second check ensures that a word has no more than two consecutive vowels. The results are stored in a struct and converted to a JSON data. Finally, it writes the JSON data to a file called "spelling_and_vowel_results.json". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


