Can you explain the difference between supervised and unsupervised machine learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Supervised machine learning involves training a model on a labeled dataset, where each input has a corresponding output. The goal is to learn a mapping function that can accurately predict the output for new inputs. On the other hand, unsupervised machine learning involves training a model on an unlabeled dataset, where the goal is to find patterns or structure in the data without any pre-existing knowledge of how the output should look like. In unsupervised learning, the model is not given any specific task or objective and must discover meaningful representations of the data on its own. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
