What is the difference between a permutation and a combination?
Hint: Both permutations and combinations are ways of selecting objects from a set, but the order of selection matters in one case and not the other.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The main difference between permutations and combinations is that in permutations, the order of selection matters, whereas in combinations, it does not. 
For example, if you have a set of 3 letters {A,B,C}, there are 6 possible permutations: ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA. Each of these represents a different order of the same set of letters. 
On the other hand, there are only 3 possible combinations of this set, since order does not matter: AB, AC, BC. <|endoftext|>
