How do scientists determine the age of fossils and rocks using radioactive dating techniques? 
Hint: Radioactive dating involves measuring the decay of unstable isotopes over time, which allows scientists to estimate how long ago an organism or rock formed.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Radioactive dating is a technique that scientists use to determine the age of fossils and rocks. It involves measuring the decay of unstable isotopes over time, which allows scientists to estimate how long ago the organism or rock formed. 
The basic idea is that certain elements in the fossil or rock will decay over time into other elements. The rate at which this decay occurs is known as the half-life of the element. By measuring the ratio of the original element to the decay product, scientists can calculate how many half-lives have passed since the sample was formed. 
For example, carbon-14 is commonly used to date organic material. Carbon-14 has a half-life of 5,700 years, so by measuring the amount of carbon-14 remaining in a sample and comparing it to the amount of stable carbon-12, scientists can estimate how long ago the organism died. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
