How can I use Go to generate dialogue lines for a character named Jack in proper screenplay format, while ensuring that each line is at most 25 words long? Can you provide an example of how to create three different dialogue lines for Jack using your function, with the constraint that each line must contain at least one punctuation mark? Here is an example Go code that generates the dialogue lines in proper screenplay format with these requirements:
func generateDialogueLine(name string, line string) string {
 punctuations := []string{".", "?", "!", ";"}
 words := strings.Fields(line)
 var newLine string
 newLine += words[0]
 for i := 1; i < len(words); i++ {
 if (len(newLine + " " + words[i]) > 25) || !containsAny(punctuations, words[i]) {
 newLine += "."
 fmt.Printf("%s: "%s"n", name, newLine)
 newLine = words[i]
 } else {
 newLine += " " + words[i]
 }
 }
 newLine += "."
 return fmt.Sprintf("%s: "%s"", name, newLine)
}
func containsAny(arr []string, str string) bool {
 for _, a := range arr {
 if strings.Contains(str, a) {
 return true
 }
 }
 return false
}
jackLine1 := generateDialogueLine("Jack", "I can't believe we're doing this, but it's our only option.")
jackLine2 := generateDialogueLine("Jack", "I'm not sure we're going to make it, but we have to try.")
jackLine3 := generateDialogueLine("Jack", "This is it, the moment we've been waiting for.")
fmt.Println(jackLine1)
fmt.Println(jackLine2)
fmt.Println(jackLine3)
This function takes in two parameters - the character name and the dialogue line - and returns a string in the proper screenplay format. The example code uses the function to generate three different dialogue lines for Jack, with the added constraint that each line must contain at least one punctuation mark and be at most 25 words long, and prints them to the console.
