What is an abstract class in object-oriented programming?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
An abstract class is a class in object-oriented programming that cannot be instantiated on its own, but instead serves as a base or blueprint for other classes to derive from. It is used to define a common interface and set of behaviors for a group of related classes.

An abstract class typically contains one or more abstract methods, which are declared but not implemented in the abstract class. These methods must be implemented in any concrete subclass that derives from the abstract class. This allows the subclass to provide its own implementation of the abstract method, while still adhering to the common interface defined by the abstract class.

Here's an example of an abstract class in Python:
```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Animal(ABC):
 @abstractmethod
 def sound(self):
 pass

class Dog(Animal):
 def sound(self):
 print("Woof!")

class Cat(Animal):
 def sound(self):
 print("Meow!")

d = Dog()
d.sound() # prints "Woof!"

c = Cat()
c.sound() # prints "Meow!"
```
