<|asst|>
Of course! In computer programming, both stacks and queues are abstract data types that allow for the storage of elements with a specific order. The main difference between them is the way they handle adding and removing elements.

A stack follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle, meaning that the last element added to the stack will be the first one removed. This is similar to stacking plates on top of each other - you can only remove the top plate.

A queue, on the other hand, follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle, meaning that the first element added to the queue will be the first one removed. This is similar to a line at a store or a bank - the person who gets in line first will be the first one served.

