Absolutely! To make this recipe vegan-friendly, simply swap out the udon noodles for soba noodles or rice noodles since they are typically vegan. Additionally, you can use a vegan-friendly version of harissa paste that does not contain any animal products. As for a substitute for kimchi, you can try using sauerkraut or pickled vegetables for a similar tangy flavor.

Alternatively, if you're looking to add some protein to the dish, you could use tofu instead of the quinoa. Simply press the tofu to remove any excess liquid, cut it into cubes, and stir-fry it with the noodles.

Here's the modified recipe:
Ingredients:
- Soba noodles
- Tofu
- Vegan-friendly harissa paste
- Sauerkraut or pickled vegetables
- Mango

Instructions:
1. Cook the soba noodles according to package instructions and set aside.
2. In a small bowl, mix together one tablespoon of vegan-friendly harissa paste with two tablespoons of water to create a sauce.
3. Cut the tofu into cubes and stir-fry in a large pan over medium-high heat until golden brown. Remove from the pan and set aside.
4. In the same pan, heat up a tablespoon of oil over medium-high heat. Add the cooked soba noodles to the pan and stir-fry for 2-3 minutes.
5. Add the harissa sauce to the pan and stir-fry for an additional minute.
6. Serve the stir-fry in bowls, topped with a spoonful of sauerkraut or pickled vegetables and diced mango.

As for incorporating a photo of the finished dish using programming language, you can use the same Python code snippet I provided earlier. Simply replace the image file name with the name of your new image file. <|endoftext|>
